You'll have to figure out by listening exactly what's so interesting about this troop.

104
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Then I'll be back to tell you how to keep in touch.

105
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,000
But in the meantime, let's get started, shall we?

106
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
The Scout movement is forming a personal tie between the different foreign countries,

107
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,000
a living force, a great brotherhood of service. a joyous work.

108
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
So today I'm talking with Jeff Kvistad.

109
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
He is the Scout Master of Troop 965..

110
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,000
How are you doing today, Jeff?

111
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Great

112
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Jeff. I wanted to ask you a few questions about your troop.

113
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
You have kind of an unusual troop, don't you?

114
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Yeah, we do.

115
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Let me start by asking you this:

116
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
What's the average temperature when you guys go out camping?

117
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Well, it depends on the time of year.

118
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:28,000
In the later months of spring, like April and May, it could be up to 110 degrees,

119
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:35,000
And our September, October campouts can be about 110, 115.

120
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:43,000
But in December and January the high could be about 65 and low about 35.

121
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
That's a pretty big swing there.

122
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
That's pretty interesting.

123
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
About how many Scouts in your troop:

124
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Well, we've been having about 16 active Scouts, but three just recently joined,

125
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Okay,

126
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
And these Scouts are kind of all from the same neighborhood.

127
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:08,000
Well, now they are, but they come from really all over the world.

128
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
Our senior patrol leader is a New Zealander,

129
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
We have an Australian,

130
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:20,000
So we've got Lebanese, French-speaking Scouts.

131
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,000
We have Dutch Scouts now who speak Dutch at home,

132
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And Americans- just a few Americans. we are definitely in the minority, though,

133
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
But they all like the BSA program

134
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And the parents like the BSA experience of having the boys learn these leadership skills.

135
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Your district and council services. about how far away are you from?

136
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:54,000
We're about a 14-hour flight from our council headquarters.

137
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Okay,

138
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
So everybody did you hear that-

139
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,000
About a 14-hour flight.

140
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
So if you have to drive for 20 minutes to get to your council office,

141
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I don't think you have any way to complain now because Jeff's quite far away.

142
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So, Jeff, we've been playing a little game here.

143
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Where in the world are you?

144
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:17,000
I am located in Kuwait between Saudi Arabia and Iraq,

145
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:24,000
And you have a Boy Scout troop that is registered with the BSA in Kuwait.

146
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Yes, we do.

147
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
So how did you find yourself in Kuwait?

148
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Well, kind of a long story.

149
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:37,000
I'm in banking and 23-year career in a bank in Chicago

150
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And in the fall of 2009, I got an email from a friend.

151
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
He had had an international career his entire life

152
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
And he sent me an email saying he was in Kuwait.

153
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
They were looking for other Westerners

154
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
And he came home and talked to my wife

155
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
And she was up for an adventure.

156
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
So by the spring of 2010,, we had moved our family

157
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
with four of our kids here to Kuwait.

158
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Tell me a little bit about the country.

159
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:09,000
I think most of us are only familiar with it through the Gulf War

160
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
and the things that we read in the news.

161
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
But what's it like living there?

162
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Yeah, I would describe Kuwait as a combination of first-world wealth

163
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:21,000
and third-world or developing world poverty.

164
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:26,000
The first-world wealth comes from the oil industry.

165
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
It pumps over 2 million barrels a day

166
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
And all that money comes into the government

167
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And they distribute that money pretty generously

168
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
to the native or ethnic Kuwaitis that are here

169
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:44,000
through government jobs, generous grants, things like that

170
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
And they in turn hire South Asians primarily

171
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
to do the cooking, cleaning, constructing.

172
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
There's a lot of folks here who come from pretty poor

173
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:01,000
circumstances back in India, Bangladesh, Pakistan

174
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and the Philippines come here for the jobs.

175
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
I think it's kind of interesting to people

176
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
that you can have a Boy Scouts of America troop

177
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,000
in another country.

178
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Direct services are counsel,

179
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:18,000
And that is right. at BSA headquarters in Texas

180
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,000
There is a transatlantic council of the BSA

181
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
that's located headquartered in Great Britain

182
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And I believe they are the counsel

183
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
primarily for the European countries.

184
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
People who are citizens of other countries-

185
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
you mentioned New Zealand as being one-

186
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
can actually join the Boy Scouts of America.

187
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
The only rule that we've found is that

188
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,000
citizens of the host country should not be joining

189
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
the Boy Scouts of America.

190
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
So, in other words, if you're a Kuwaiti citizen,

191
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
your first obligation is to the Kuwait Boy Scouts Association,

192
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
And Kuwait does have one.

193
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
It should be fine to register expats,

194
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:08,000
all kinds of expats, into your BSA troop.

195
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
An expat is

196
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
All the non-citizens of that country

197
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
who are living in that country. expatriates,

198
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Okay, expatriates, okay.

199
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:25,000
And so I guess a couple of practical questions

200
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,000
come to mind.

201
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
For instance, to join the Boy Scouts,

202
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
you need to learn the Pledge of Allegiance

203
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and things like that.

204
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
How is that handled?

205
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Yeah, how we've handled it is to become a scout.

206
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
All we've said is: you have to know

207
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
what the American Pledge of Allegiance says.

208
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:52,000
We don't ask them to make it a personal commitment themselves.

209
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:56,000
And then when we have our opening flag ceremony

210
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
at each of our troop meetings- which we do-

211
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
and we display the American flag-

212
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
our senior patrol leader-

213
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,000
because he's not an American, he's a New Zealander-

214
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
he stands aside and says:

215
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
all of you who are Americans,

216
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
please follow our assistant senior patrol leader,

217
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
who is an American, in the Pledge of Allegiance.

218
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
And we also qualify it by saying:

219
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:24,000
if you are not an American, please stand respectfully.

220
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
We talked a little bit about the temperatures,

221
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
of where you go camping.

222
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
Now in the States, typically

223
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
we're headed off to the woods,

224
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
but it's a little bit of a different environment.

225
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Yeah, it's complete desert.

226
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
We go out.

227
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:43,000
There's no trees, no grass, no shrubs. around

228
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:47,000
My very first camp out, we went out to a place

229
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
we call Volcano Ridge.

230
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
It has really nothing relating to a volcano,

231
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
It's just some mounds in the desert.

232
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
But we arrived at night

233
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
so I couldn't really see around me.

234
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
But in the morning, when I woke up,

235
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
looked outside my tent,

236
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
it looked like I was on the moon-

237
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Just nothing but sand and gravel and rocks

238
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
for as far as the eye could see.

239
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
So not a whole lot of pioneering,

240
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
Not a whole lot.

241
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
We have to buy our own poles.

242
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:27,000
What about things like campfires and stuff like that?

243
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
We usually bring our own wood out,

244
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:33,000
moving pallets that are used in warehouses and stuff.

245
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
You'll find those kind of strewn around construction sites

246
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and things like that.

247
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
So we pick those up, put them in our trailer

248
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
when we go out camping.

249
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Any particular dangers to camping in the desert?

250
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
I mean, it's such a foreign environment

251
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
for most of the people who'd be listening to this.

252
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
This year began really tragically

253
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,000
when we started our camp out with the news

254
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:04,000
that a high school girl had died

255
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
taking a hike with another student organization here.

256
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
Originally we thought the reports were

257
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
it was a scorpion sting,

258
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
but with further information

259
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
it was most likely due to dehydration-

260
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:22,000
Cardiac arrest from dehydration.

261
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:27,000
So our camping year began with that news

262
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,000
And so obviously we were all concerned,

263
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Reminded us all that the desert is a deadly place

264
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
or can be a deadly place.

265
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:41,000
So what we did is as any scout should.

266
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
we managed our risk wisely.

267
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,000
So we made an immediate rule

268
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
that all scouts had to bring a camel back

269
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
or some equivalent type of device,

270
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
And we found that really does make a difference.

271
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Scouts do sip more water throughout the day

272
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
if they're wearing it.

273
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:06,000
So it's definitely tragic what happened this year,

274
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
but it was a good lesson for all of us

275
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
that we do need to be careful.

276
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:15,000
So, being this far away from the resources

277
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
that we're used to-

278
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
the support resources of a council and a district

279
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:21,000
and things like that-

280
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
do you find that particularly challenging?

281
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
Yes, it is challenging

282
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:29,000
because we don't have the training close by.

283
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:33,000
Like you said, we can't go to a camp

284
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:37,000
just half an hour away.

285
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:41,000
The Transatlantic Council in Great Britain

286
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:46,000
has been very generous in inviting us to their events, however,

287
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:51,000
And to our senior patrol leader

288
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,000
and assistant senior patrol leader this year

289
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,000
went to NYLT in Great Britain last summer

290
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
and that was a great experience for them.

291
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Did that help build the troops on?

292
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Oh, it was tremendous, tremendous difference.

293
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:10,000
I mean all the troop committee members commented

294
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:14,000
the parents noticed that between last year and this year

295
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
the boys really got it

296
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,000
And the parents who went to that- one parent in particular

297
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
who expected to go to this camp,

298
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:27,000
expecting to be one of the leaders

299
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:31,000
and found himself sitting on his behind. he said,

300
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
And he said he finally got it.

301
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
His eyes were opened about what scouting was all about-

302
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
that it's the boys taking the lead.

303
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:44,000
So it was really a transformational experience.

304
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:48,000
It really elevated our troop to the next level.

305
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:51,000
Usually, when we find ourselves with some challenges,

306
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,000
we also find ourselves with some opportunities.

307
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:59,000
Being in Kuwait and being able to camp in the desert

308
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:03,000
and stuff like that is quite an opportunity.

309
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:08,000
What kind of other things are you looking at in the future,

310
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Like some of our dreams are?

311
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:16,000
we were talking about a hike in Wadi Rum in Jordan this year,

312
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
actually like a 50-miler,

313
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
But right now we've tabled that idea for next year.

314
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Other ideas are an all-night hike

315
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:31,000
and then watching the sunrise over Mount Sinai in Egypt.

316
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Our schools aren't all on the same vacation schedule,

317
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:39,000
So we have to deal with passports and visas

318
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
And then the cost.

319
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:47,000
We have to rely on donations from corporations or families.

320
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:50,000
What I have found when I've traveled internationally

321
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:54,000
is that there's just not a whole lot that separates us all.

322
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,000
There's some cultural things, there's some societal things,

323
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
but when it gets right down to it-

324
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
sit down with a cup of coffee with somebody,

325
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
you're pretty much all speaking the same language.

326
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
After you grow up in the States,

327
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
more or less by an accident of geography,

328
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:12,000
you don't really get to interact with a whole lot of people from other countries.

329
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:16,000
It's fascinating to be in a place where you're an American

330
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
and you're in the minority nationally.

331
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
It just gives you a new perspective, doesn't it?

332
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
It's a great perspective too,

333
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:30,000
because you realize then what minorities experience in America.

334
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:33,000
So it's very much a learning experience

335
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:36,000
for my wife and I and our kids,

336
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
And we appreciate being Americans.

337
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:45,000
We appreciate freedoms and the institutions

338
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:50,000
that we have in America that give America such stability.

339
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:53,000
And probably one of the biggest things I've learned

340
00:20:53,000 --> 00:21:00,000
is that, despite the politics that we see in the news,

341
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:05,000
that generally people really love the United States.

342
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
They love Americans.

343
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:11,000
And I'm going to go back. I know

344
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:14,000
more appreciative of what we have in America,

345
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:18,000
but also very appreciative of these different cultures

346
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:22,000
that I've come to know and respect here in the Middle East.

347
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
Is there any particular thing you can point to

348
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:28,000
that you've picked up specifically from that culture?

349
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:31,000
that was a big change for you.

350
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:37,000
Among the Middle Easterners and the South Asians that are here,

351
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:42,000
there is a warmth that is very refreshing.

352
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:46,000
We Americans tend to be so business-like

353
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:52,000
And over here you can sense that among different cultures-

354
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:55,000
and I should qualify that with different cultures-

355
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:59,000
have different degrees of warmth that you can sense,

356
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:06,000
but there is a genuine human warmth. that is delightful.

357
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:09,000
Kuwait is a majority Muslim country, is it?

358
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:10,000
Yes, it is.

359
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:11,000
What's that like?

360
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:16,000
There's more of a social recognition here of religion,

361
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:22,000
and Islam is very structured in the sense of five times a day

362
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:26,000
we hear the call to prayer from the minarets,

363
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:31,000
which is something from an American perspective we're not used to.

364
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:34,000
We have scouts, a number of scouts, who are Muslim.

365
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:41,000
There is a religious emblem in BSA for Islamic scouts.

366
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:45,000
What we've done is we still pray out loud,

367
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:50,000
but we always stick to the non-sectarian prayers.

368
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
We say, like film on grace

369
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:57,000
Film scouts, whether it's okay to pray out loud in this kind of setting,

370
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,000
and they've assured us that it's fine.

371
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:06,000
This year we selected our chaplain's aide as a Muslim scout.

372
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:11,000
Our chaplain we recently got is a Christian,

373
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:17,000
So we've kept the reverence part in our troupe. very much so,

374
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:23,000
but also being very sensitive to our differences in faith as well,

375
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:27,000
I would imagine you find that more enriching than challenging.

376
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
Yes, it is enriching.

377
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:35,000
And again, by camping with scouts of different faiths,

378
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
you realize how much you have in common,

379
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:42,000
how much we need to depend on each other to make a camp successful

380
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:49,000
and how much of the 12 points of the scout law still hold in common,

381
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:52,000
and very much so that all unites us.

382
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:58,000
The audience we're talking to is mostly scouts in the States.

383
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
Maybe they're sitting here listening and they're thinking

384
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:04,000
it would be nice for us to be able to get into a situation like that

385
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
or to be able to see a little bit more of the world.

386
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:13,000
How would we encourage them to look at the prospect of an international trip?

387
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
Do you think that's something you would suggest to them?

388
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
Oh, absolutely.

389
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:26,000
Americans, I think, have a very limited view of the world through their TV set.

390
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:32,000
Nothing gets on the TV unless something bad happens there.

391
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:38,000
Americans, I think, tend to think the world is a very scary place,

392
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
particularly in the Middle East.

393
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:46,000
When we came here, alarm bells sounded throughout our extended families.

394
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:51,000
Why would we even want to be this close to Iraq?

395
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:56,000
But my wife, fortunately, is very adventurous type and I am too.

396
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:01,000
What you find is that people are the same all over the world.

397
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Generally, people want to live in peace,

398
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
They want to have a decent job.

399
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
They want to raise their families.

400
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,000
People still go shopping here.

401
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
We've got shopping malls,

402
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:15,000
We've got traffic,

403
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:19,000
We've got traffic jams.

404
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:27,000
In some ways, the world has definitely become a global village.

405
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:30,000
Sometimes I feel like I'm still in the States.

406
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:34,000
We had occasion to do any events with Kuwaiti Scouts

407
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:37,000
or any other scouting organizations over there.

408
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:40,000
We did have an event with the Kuwaiti Scouts.

409
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:46,000
We found some challenges, though, with language, with age differences.

410
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:51,000
They allow scouts here up into the early 20s.

411
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
We made the best of it though.

412
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
We had a weekend camp out with them

413
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:58,000
and we did some games, some competitions.

414
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
Jeff, how long have you been a scout master?

415
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
Only a year,

416
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:06,000
I imagine. your experience would probably be pretty similar

417
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:10,000
to any scout master who's been around for about a year or so.

418
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Both my son and I were new to Scouts. two years ago,

419
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:18,000
A friend of ours introduced us to scouting

420
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:21,000
and we immediately took to it.

421
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
We were hooked on it.

422
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,000
However, there's a lot of transitions here. in Kuwait,

423
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
Families come and go.

424
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:33,000
In the fall of 2011,, we found ourselves

425
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
our troop without a scout master

426
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:38,000
because our previous scout master had to move on.

427
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
He was reassigned

428
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:45,000
By January 2012,. our troop had to recharge.

429
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:50,000
We could not recharge without a scout master.

430
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
All the parents got together

431
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:57,000
and we were all facing: what are we going to do

432
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
without a scout master?

433
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:02,000
They all took a step back

434
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:05,000
and I forgot to take a step back.

435
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:10,000
Lo and behold, I was the scout master

436
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
by unanimous consent,

437
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:16,000
Without any really previous scouting experience.

438
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:20,000
in my youth, or up till two years ago,

439
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:25,000
I was a scout master and it's been a great experience-

440
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:27,000
A great experience in leadership.

441
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:31,000
You learn pretty fast that you can adjust your expectations.

442
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:35,000
You should not have expectations that you've grown to have

443
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:38,000
with adults in the corporate world.

444
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:42,000
What I found difficult in being a scout master

445
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:47,000
is when to intervene and when not to.

446
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:51,000
When is that judgment?

447
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:54,000
I suppose it's where the art comes in.

448
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:58,000
It's not a science, but when is it mentoring

449
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:01,000
and when is it interfering?

450
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:04,000
That's a hard one to figure out.

451
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:07,000
The great thing about your podcast-

452
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:10,000
and I've listened to 46 of them now-

453
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:15,000
your consistent theme is to let the boys lead.

454
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
I try to do that more and more,

455
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
and every podcast I listen to

456
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:24,000
is further inspiration for me to do that.

457
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:27,000
However, I know there's times I have to mentor

458
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,000
and I know there's times I have to intervene.

459
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:34,000
It's deciding when to do that.

460
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
That's the most difficult.

461
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:39,000
Everybody who's listening is who's a scout master

462
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
is nodding their head right now,

463
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:44,000
because it's always a challenge.

464
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:47,000
You certainly don't feel alone.

465
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:53,000
No, I realize that your podcast theme

466
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,000
and your philosophy is there

467
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
because everybody experiences it.

468
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
I certainly have enjoyed talking with you.

469
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
I really appreciate you agreeing to do this.

470
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
I know it's fascinating for people to learn

471
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
about what happens in the rest of the world.

472
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
It's been a great experience for us

473
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:11,000
and our kids too, even at the times

474
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:14,000
they wish they were back in the States.

475
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
I think they're going to look back on this time

476
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:19,000
and really see how they've grown,

477
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
really in seeing other cultures,

478
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:24,000
other ways of life here.

479
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
The time I asked you for about an hour-

480
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
and I know you have other things

481
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
that you've got to move on to,

482
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
As do I, and I really appreciate

483
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
you spending the time with me. Jeff,

484
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:38,000
It's been nice to talk to you.

485
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
You're welcome, Clark.

486
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:44,000
Your blog and your podcast are tremendous,

487
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
tremendous help.

488
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:48,000
I know I told you this.

489
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:51,000
It's been the wood badge. I haven't been able to attend yet.

490
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,000
Don't worry,

491
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:55,000
I'll attend it someday, I hope.

492
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:59,000
But keep up the great work that you're doing.

493
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:00,000
I appreciate it.

494
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:03,000
I'm glad you're finding things helpful.

495
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,000
Once again, thanks, Jeff

496
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:09,000
Ma'asalama, which means peace. be with you in Arabic,

497
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
Asalama.

498
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
Well, thank you so much for listening

499
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:40,000
to this edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

500
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,000
Here's how to keep in touch.

501
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,000
Read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmastercgcom.

